1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
The memory you trust most is your least reliable one.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,920
Not the vague ones. The vivid one.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,920
The one you can describe in order, with the lines and the tone and the look on their face.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,920
That memory has been edited the most, and you were the editor.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,920
You have an explanation ready. Good memory.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,920
But confidence does not track accuracy.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,920
It tracks rehearsal. Your memory is not a recording.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,920
It is reconstruction, rebuilt from scratch every time you reach for it, and every retelling is a save,

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,920
not a read. So which of yours have been rewritten?

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,920
You cannot tell from the inside. By the end you will know why your brain was built this way on purpose, who is feeding it now,

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,920
and the one question that tells you when to trust yourself.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,920
Rehearsal is only one of the ways new material gets into a memory.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,920
The first hard proof came from something far smaller than a retelling.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,920
One word. In 1974, two psychologists named Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,920
sat students down and showed them films of car accidents.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,920
Short films, a few seconds each.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,920
Then they asked everyone the same question with one deliberate difference buried inside it.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,920
About how fast were the cars going when they smashed into each other?

19
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,920
For another group the verb was not smashed.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,920
It was contacted. Same film.

21
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,920
Same students. One verb.

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,920
The group that got smashed estimated just under forty one miles an hour.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,920
The group that got contacted estimated just under thirty two.

24
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,920
Nine miles an hour of difference, produced by a single word inside a question asked after the event was already over.

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,920
Nobody was arguing. Nobody was being pressured.

26
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,920
They were just reporting what they saw. Then Loftus and Palmer did the part that matters more.

27
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,920
They ran it again, with a film of a multi-car crash, split the room the same way, and sent everyone home.

28
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,920
A week later they brought them back and asked a question that had nothing to do with speed.

29
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,920
Did you see any broken glass? There was no broken glass in that film.

30
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,920
None. Of the people who had been given the word smashed, about a third said yes, they had seen it.

31
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,920
Of the people given hit, about one in seven.

32
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,920
The word had not changed their opinion or their guess or their willingness to please a researcher.

33
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,920
It had changed the film.

34
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,920
Seven days later they were looking at a slightly different accident, one with glass on the road, and the glass had arrived through a question.

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,920
This was not new, and Loftus was building on someone who had seen it forty years earlier.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,920
In 1932 a Cambridge psychologist named Frederic Bartlett gave English students a folk tale from

37
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,920
a culture they knew nothing about, full of canoes and seal hunting and spirits, and asked them to retell it.

38
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,920
Then he had it retold again, and again, down a chain of people.

39
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,920
The story did not simply fade. It reorganised itself.

40
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,920
Canoes turned into boats. Seal hunting turned into fishing.

41
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,920
The supernatural parts quietly disappeared.

42
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,920
Every version was shorter, tidier, and more English than the one before, and

43
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,920
every single person believed they were repeating what they had been given.

44
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,920
Bartlett's conclusion was that remembering is not retrieval at all.

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,920
It is imaginative reconstruction.

46
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,920
You do not open a file. You take a handful of fragments, a feeling, a rough shape, and you rebuild the event

47
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,920
using whatever else you know, and the rebuild is so fast and so seamless that it arrives in your mind wearing the

48
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,920
costume of a recording. And it can be built out of almost nothing.

49
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,920
In 1995 Loftus and Jacqueline Pickrell gave people four

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,920
short stories about their own childhoods, collected from a family member.

51
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,920
Three were true. One was invented: you were lost in a shopping mall at around five years old, crying,

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,920
and an elderly stranger found you and brought you back.

53
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,920
Roughly one in four came to remember it.

54
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,920
Not vaguely. Some of them added details nobody had supplied, the stranger's flannel shirt,

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,920
what the shop fronts looked like, how frightened they had been.

56
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,920
When they were finally told which memory was planted, some of them were genuinely reluctant to give it up.

57
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,920
Which raises something uncomfortable about your own certainty tonight.

58
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,920
You have no way to audit your own footage.

59
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,920
There is no footage. Before you decide your brain is faulty, look at the world it was built for,

60
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,920
because this design is not an accident and it is not damage.

61
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,920
For almost the whole of human existence there was no writing.

62
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,920
Two hundred thousand years of people, and roughly five thousand years of anything you could read.

63
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,920
So for something like ninety seven percent of your species' time on earth, everything a group knew lived only in living memory and

64
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,920
moved only by being spoken out loud.

65
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,920
So what is a memory system for in a world like that?

66
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,920
Not an archive. An archive is pointless

67
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,920
when there is nothing to check it against and nobody who will ever read it.

68
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,920
What it is, in that world, is a transmission device.

69
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,920
Its job is to hold the useful shape of what happened and hand it to the next person in a form they can actually use.

70
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,920
Accuracy of wording is worth nothing.

71
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,920
Usefulness is worth everything.

72
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,920
The anthropologist Jack Goody spent years with the LoDagaa of northern Ghana, recording the Bagre,

73
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,920
a long sacred recitation performed at initiation.

74
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,920
Everyone in that world understood it as fixed.

75
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,920
Ancient, handed down whole, the same every time.

76
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,920
Goody recorded it more than once, from the same men, and the versions did not match.

77
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,920
Different lengths. Different episodes.

78
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,920
Details that appeared in one telling and were simply absent from another.

79
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,920
Nobody was lying to him. They were reconstructing, and from the inside

80
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,920
reconstruction feels precisely like remembering.

81
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,920
Albert Lord, who with Milman Parry recorded the great epic singers of Yugoslavia, ran into the same wall.

82
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,920
The singers told him plainly that they sang each song exactly as they had learned it, word for word, line for line.

83
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,920
Then Lord recorded the same singer performing the same song years apart, and the songs came out at different lengths

84
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,920
with scenes that had moved or vanished. The claim of perfect fidelity was completely sincere.

85
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,920
The fidelity was not there. Now here is the part that turns the Loftus experiment upside down.

86
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,920
In a band of thirty people, new information about a past event had exactly one possible source.

87
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,920
The others who were there. Ten people watched the same hunt from ten different positions and each of them saw a fragment.

88
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,920
The only way that band ever assembled one good picture of what happened was for the fragments to move between them and

89
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,920
settle into each person's memory. Which means a memory that absorbs what it is told afterwards is not a broken memory.

90
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,920
It is the pooling mechanism. Suggestion is how a group with no writing arrived at a shared and usually more

91
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,920
accurate version of events than any single pair of eyes could produce.

92
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,920
And there was a second reason to leave the door open. The world kept changing and nothing wrote it down.

93
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,920
The river shifted course. The herd took a different route.

94
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,920
The plant that was safe last generation grew somewhere else now.

95
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,920
A memory that held rigidly to the oldest version of the world eventually killed someone.

96
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,920
A memory that quietly took on the newest thing it heard carried the current version.

97
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,920
In a world without records, the freshest information in the room was almost always better than the oldest information in your head.

98
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,920
So your ancestors were not fighting their own suggestibility.

99
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,920
They were living off it. The band that remembered flexibly ate, and passed the trait to you.

100
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,920
Now open your phone. The door is still wide open.

101
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,920
It has never closed. But almost nothing coming through it now is another witness pooling what they saw.

102
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,920
It is a lawyer's question with the answer folded inside it.

103
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,920
It is a headline that tells you what the footage means before you watch the footage.

104
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,920
It is the group chat where somebody else's version of the evening arrives before you have finished forming your own.

105
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,920
It is a comment telling you what you must have felt.

106
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,920
It is the same clip served to you thirty times until the edited version is smoother and more available than

107
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,920
the thing you actually witnessed. That is the mismatch.

108
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,920
Not that your memory updates, it always did, and it kept people alive.

109
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:39,920
It is that the update channel used to be fed by thirty people who were standing right there and had no reason to distort anything,

110
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,920
and now it is fed by strangers, headlines, feeds, and people with an argument to win.

111
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,920
And the closest example is not a courtroom.

112
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,920
It is the story you tell about someone you know.

113
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,920
Count the times you have told it. Six, maybe eleven.

114
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,920
Each time to someone slightly different, and each time you shaped it a little for the person listening, sharpened the part that landed,

115
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,920
dropped the part that slowed it down, gave yourself a slightly better line and them a slightly worse one.

116
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,920
And every one of those tellings was not a read.

117
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,920
It was a save. You were not consulting the memory.

118
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,920
You were writing over it. So here is the shift, and it is one small move.

119
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,920
The next time you are completely certain about something that matters, do not ask whether it is true.

120
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,920
Ask how many times you have told it. Because confidence does not track accuracy.

121
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:38,920
Confidence tracks rehearsal. The memory you can describe in the most vivid detail, in the smoothest order, with the best lines,

122
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,920
is not your most reliable memory. It is your most edited one.

123
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,920
The smoothness is the tell.

124
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,920
Real events come back rough. That does not mean stop trusting yourself.

125
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,920
It means separate the two things Bartlett separated.

126
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,920
The gist survives. The details drift.

127
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,920
So hold the gist with both hands and hand the details back.

128
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,920
Something happened and it hurt is almost certainly true.

129
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:10,920
He said those exact words, in that exact tone, while looking at me like that is a sentence that has been through eleven drafts,

130
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,920
and you were the editor every time.

131
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,920
And when the other person remembers it differently, they are not lying to you.

132
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:24,920
That is the relief in all of this. There are two reconstruction devices in the room, each rebuilding from fragments,

133
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,920
each shaped by different retellings to different audiences, both running honestly.

134
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,920
Nobody has the recording. There is no recording.

135
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,920
So tonight, just this. The broken glass was never in the film.

136
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,920
It was in the question. Nobody lied to those students and nobody was weak or gullible.

137
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:49,920
Somebody handed them one word, and their mind built glass out of it, and a week later they could see it and describe it

138
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,920
and would have sworn to it in front of anyone. You are carrying glass like that right now,

139
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,920
somewhere in the story you tell about a person, or about yourself.

140
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,920
You are certain you saw it. Somebody handed it to you, and you kept it, and you have been paying for it

141
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,920
ever since as if you found it yourself.

142
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,920
The memory you trust most is not the one that happened.

143
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,920
It is the one you have handled the most.

144
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,920
And notice that the same machine does not only rebuild the past that happened, it builds the one that did not,

145
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,920
and then hands you the bill for the difference.

146
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:28,920
So one last question before you go. Have you ever missed something by almost nothing, a job that came down to two people,

147
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:35,920
a train by a single minute, and found that it hurt far more than all the things you missed by a mile?

148
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:40,920
If that is you, you are not ungrateful and you are not a sore loser.

149
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,920
There is a reason the near miss burns hotter than the far one, it was measured straight off the faces of Olympic

150
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,920
medalists with the sound turned off, and there is one question you can ask that gets the invented version out of the room.

151
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,920
That is the video on your screen right now.

152
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,920
Why you would be happier coming third than second.

153
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,500
Watch that one next.
